//
// Probe: F33. PartialEq is asymmetric across int/float, and neither of the
// two obvious symmetrisations is a valid equivalence relation.
//
// `a b ==` computes b.eq(a): the word pulls the top of the stack first and
// that operand becomes the receiver. So the ORDER OF THE LITERALS decides
// which direction is exercised, and both must be written out.
//
"probe: the asymmetry itself" println
"  int on top -> I64.eq(F64), truncates the float ->" println 42.5 42 == println
"  float on top -> F64.eq(I64), widens the int    ->" println 42 42.5 == println

"probe: truncation is not transitive" println
"  42 == 42.5 ->" println 42.5 42 == println
"  42 == 42.9 ->" println 42.9 42 == println
"  42.5 == 42.9 ->" println 42.9 42.5 == println

"probe: above 2^53, both directions, so both are pinned" println
"  int on top, truncating  (2^53+1).eq(2^53.0) ->" println 9007199254740992.0 9007199254740993 == println
"  float on top, widening  (2^53.0).eq(2^53+1) ->" println 9007199254740993 9007199254740992.0 == println
